WEBVTT

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.550
Even though victory is 
in reach for Fake Akagi,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.550
he lets it slip away at the last moment.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:21.180
The wager is doubled from 
16 million to 32 million.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:23.230
Just as Akagi predicted,

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.320
Fake Akagi is pushed further and 
further into the corner by Urabe.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.230
<b>Episode 11</b>

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.230
<b>The Foundation for Despair</b>

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.470
East Hand 1, ninth turn.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.210
Can we call in the real Akagi?

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.040
This man is no good.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.950
There's something he lacks.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.080
Something Fake Akagi lacks?

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.090
Yes. He's missing the most crucial 
quality that gamblers must have.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.750
He doesn't have a sense of the moment.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.590
A sense of the moment?

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.500
Pinfu, tsumo. 700, 400.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.470
What he believes in is the 
ideology known as probability.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.970
But when 32 million is riding 
on a single East-South match,

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.580
you need something more 
than just probability.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.520
The ability to sniff out the tiles.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:27.590
At this point, he needs to be able 
to pick them with pinpoint accuracy.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:28.920
But that's...

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.830
He needs to be able to 
do that to be relevant.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.280
All he can do is make shallow calculations.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.830
He won't be able to win like that.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.140
East Hand 2, 14th turn.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.570
Fake Akagi is in tenpai.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.180
<i>If I go for a double pon wait on 
the 3 of bamboo and 7 of circles,</i>

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.170
<i>drawing it myself will get me a san ankou.</i>

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:55.320
<i>But considering that the 
7 of circles is the dora,</i>

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.320
<i>a tanyao with two dora is enough.</i>

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.950
<i>A 3 of bamboo from his hand.</i>

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.180
<i>He's in tenpai.</i>

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.790
<i>But he still isn't declaring riichi.</i>

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.140
<i>I guess he concluded that it's not possible</i>

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.530
<i>given how few tiles are left.</i>

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.810
<i>That's weak, bro!</i>

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.770
<i>If you have a good hand, 
you've got to be greedy!</i>

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.680
<i>With that sort of meticulous play style,</i>

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.090
<i>you won't be able to put 
pressure on your opponents</i>

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.510
<i>or bring luck on your side.</i>

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.110
<i>Your play style is so timid.</i>

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.930
<i>I'm going to be much bolder than that.</i>

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.500
Bro, do you accept opens?

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.740
Open?

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.220
The 2 han open riichi.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.460
We do in theory.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:43.960
All right.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.460
Riichi, open!

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.280
<i>He actually did it!</i>

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.710
<i>And a double pon wait on the 
red dragon and the 3 of circles?</i>

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.080
<i>There are already two red 
dragons in the discards.</i>

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.610
<i>He's only got the 3 of circles to wait for.</i>

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.000
<i>He must be kidding.</i>

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.920
<i>There's no way that my three-way 
wait will lose to this!</i>

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:07.850
<i>Damn it!</i>

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.350
Urabe's play style is crazy.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.910
He discarded the 5 of circles 
and declared riichi,

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:16.320
which means that he could have 
had a two-sided wait, too.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.440
If he had gone for the two-sided wait, 
a tsumo would have netted him 7,800.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.740
But if he goes for the san ankou, 
with a tsumo he'll get a proper mangan.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.940
And if he gets reverse dora 
for any of his triplets,

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:32.180
opening the riichi will boost 
him to a dealer baiman.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.480
He could finish everything 
with this one hand.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.810
Plus, he probably feels it.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.720
He can smell it...

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.450
The winning tile.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.800
The premonition that he'll 
draw the 3 of circles.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.190
<i>Could it be...</i>

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:50.690
<i>Don't draw it!</i>

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:02.640
He didn't get the ippatsu, 
but in the next turn...

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:05.430
Tsumo.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.880
Open, tsumo, san ankou. Mangan.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.230
Oh, wait, I got three reverse 
dora, so it's a baiman.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.980
Sorry, bro.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.290
<i>No way.</i>

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.490
<i>This is unbelievable!</i>

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.410
Ishikawa, it's no use.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:27.970
He's out of the question.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:32.500
Letting him continue to play 
will only widen the wound.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.430
Call Akagi!

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.150
We must fight a demon with a demon!

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:43.840
Akagi!

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:45.560
You need to come right now.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.810
Our rep player is being torn apart.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.480
We need your help.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.850
Urabe started winning?

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:55.990
Yeah, that bastard got cocky 
and kept raising the wager.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.950
It's at 32 million now.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:05:59.680
Please, Akagi!

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.830
I'm sorry, but I don't feel like it.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.420
What are you talking about?

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.820
Nobody but you can do it!

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:06.930
Akagi!

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.470
What about Osamu?

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.590
You mean the kid with the freckles?

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.570
Yes, why don't you have him play?

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:15.940
That's not funny.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.080
This isn't an opponent 
an amateur can handle.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:22.880
Ishikawa-san, keep the wager secret 
from Osamu and let him play freely.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:24.820
He might do quite well.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:27.030
Quite well isn't enough, Akagi.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.870
Do what you like.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.320
If Osamu doesn't play, neither will I.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.160
Akagi...

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.000
So you'll play after Osamu?

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.060
If he plays, you play?

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.030
Well, yeah.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.800
It's East Hand 4.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:48.810
Osamu has just become dealer.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.050
<i>Is it really okay for 
me to be playing here?</i>

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:58.550
<i>Didn't they say something 
about the wager being 500,000?</i>

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.290
<i>Five hundred grand, huh?</i>

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:05.290
Osamu is in tenpai.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:06.660
<i>I'm in tenpai.</i>

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:08.410
<i>If I discard the 3 of circles,</i>

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.570
<i>I'll get a double pon wait for the 
7 of bamboo and the north wind.</i>

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.300
<i>Should I just stick with 
that since I'm the dealer?</i>

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.460
<i>But I'm 38,000 points behind first place.</i>

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.300
<i>If I play too defensively, 
I'll never take over the lead.</i>

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.410
<i>Let's play it big.</i>

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.180
<i>Ah...</i>

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:26.880
<i>Not bad.</i>

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.030
Osamu dismantles his tenpai and 
goes for a tanyao, sanshoku hand.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.880
Later, that proves to be a good call.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.150
A few turns later...

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:38.120
Tenpai.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.390
An ideal tanyao, pinfu, sanshoku.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.300
Riichi!

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:48.400
<i>Usually, people would buckle under 
the pressure of a 32 million wager.</i>

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:52.860
<i>Despite his appearance, 
he plays quite calmly.</i>

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.770
<i>I heard he was a filler until Akagi comes,</i>

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.200
<i>but it looks like he's not 
completely useless, either.</i>

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:00.730
Tsumo!

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.610
Menzen, pinfu, tsumo, sanshoku. 
Mangan! 4,000 from all!

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.620
Osamu picks up momentum with this win.

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:12.890
Moving on to bonus hand 1, 
he gets another tsumo.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.490
2,700 from all.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:21.000
Urabe, who was barely visible 
before, is now almost in reach.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:24.520
The distance is a mere 11,200.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:26.970
A mangan or higher will cause an upset.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.040
He's not bad.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:34.710
Even though entering the game dead 
last can be relaxing in a way,

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:39.490
it's like he's not affected by 
the 32 million wager whatsoever.

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.870
<i>Thirty-two million?!</i>

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.690
<i>Thirty-two million...</i>

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.740
<i>He kept quiet</i>

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.660
<i>because he figured a wager that 
high would make me nervous.</i>

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.160
<i>Thirty-two million...</i>

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.490
<i>What's going to happen if I lose?</i>

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:00.050
<i>Debt?</i>

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.660
<i>But what if I win? 
Even a 10% cut would be...</i>

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.410
<i>3.2 million.</i>

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.140
Osamu's starting hand.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:12.880
<i>The 8 of circles is the dora.</i>

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.880
<i>A tanyao with three dora will 
be enough for an upset.</i>

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:18.400
<i>I want to win with this hand.</i>

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.850
<i>If I win, I'll be in first place... if I win...</i>

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:23.590
Chi!

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:27.330
Chi!

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:28.900
<i>Not good.</i>

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.470
<i>His calm play style from before is gone.</i>

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:32.890
<i>You had luck on your side...</i>

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:35.330
<i>You should have just have kept 
playing like you have been.</i>

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.630
Osamu was in tenpai after just 6 turns...

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.810
But he can't draw this 
closed wait 4 of circles.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:43.180
He can't draw it.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:44.210
He can't draw it.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.190
And in the 10th turn, Urabe...

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:47.710
Riichi.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:51.980
Two turns later...

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:54.220
<i>Go through!</i>

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:56.210
Ron!

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.490
Riichi, honitsu, one dora. Mangan.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:01.190
<i>He got me.</i>

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:04.430
<i>He used up all of my winning tiles.</i>

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:06.530
<i>That's why he declared the riichi.</i>

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:08.390
<i>It's like he could see 
through everything I did...</i>

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.500
<i>I can't do this.</i>

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.180
Want to switch, Osamu?

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:14.670
Akagi-san!

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.780
Urabe is 28,400 points ahead.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.010
But are you sure about this?

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:33.130
There's 32 million riding on this match.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:36.130
You'll be entering in the South round, 
and almost 30,000 points behind.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.560
There's no guarantee that you will win...

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:40.300
You're so stupid.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.070
Is that what you were 
worrying so much about?

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:46.840
None of that matters.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.470
It's someone else's money.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:49.840
We can be totally carefree.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.660
Akagi-san, how could you say that?

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.400
It's simply the truth.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.110
The ones who should be nervous 
are the yakuza men behind us...

00:10:57.930 --> 00:10:58.880
and Urabe.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:01.690
Urabe?

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.140
Think about it.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:06.420
Who's the one person who can stand 
to lose this match the least?

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:10.160
Urabe's the one who raised the 
stakes to this ridiculous level.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.080
He won't get away with a

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:15.550
"Sorry, I screwed up in the 
end, and lost 32 million."

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:19.180
He'll have to pay, 
with cash or his blood.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:23.760
The pressure he's feeling 
right now must be immense.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:28.010
<i>I see.</i>

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.870
<i>Akagi's right.</i>

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:31.390
<i>I was too fixated on the point difference</i>

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:35.050
<i>and had lost sight of the crucial piece, 
the mental state of the opponent.</i>

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.050
<i>It shows again that Akagi,</i>

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.050
<i>Akagi Shigeru, is the only 
one we can rely on.</i>

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:42.040
The game resumes.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.060
South Hand 1.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.080
<i>All right, show me...</i>

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.590
<i>My first sight of Akagi Shigeru 
playing Mahjong in five years!</i>

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:50.980
<i>How is he going to play?</i>

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:58.000
But contrary to Ishikawa's expectations, 
Akagi doesn't do anything special.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:01.040
Ordinary hands.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.200
Even his single han riichi is dodged by Urabe.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:05.280
Tsumo.

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:09.380
And in South Hand 2,

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:15.620
just like before, Akagi immediately 
declares riichi after getting into tenpai.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.490
<i>Why, Akagi? Why aren't you doing anything?</i>

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:24.750
<i>Shouldn't you have at least waited 
for that hand to get better?</i>

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.210
<i>What's this kid planning?</i>

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.260
<i>It seems like a tenpai 
without any points behind it,</i>

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.480
<i>but I'll just have to play along 
and keep an eye out for now.</i>

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.980
<i>At least until I can figure 
out what he's doing.</i>

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:44.260
<i>The worst would be for him 
to have the west wind dora.</i>

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:45.720
<i>That'd be an instant mangan.</i>

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:49.920
<i>What the...</i>

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.560
<i>The dora passes?</i>

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:54.560
<i>That's the third west wind.</i>

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:57.560
<i>The possibility that he's 
got any dora is gone now.</i>

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:00.170
<i>So is he going for a 
sanshoku with the lower tiles?</i>

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:05.030
But that possibility also 
disappears as all four

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:08.140
of the 4 of bamboo and 1 of 
circles tiles appear in the discards.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:09.870
<i>In that case...</i>

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:12.590
<i>There's his winning tile!</i>

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:15.610
<i>If he gets lucky with the reverse 
dora, it could go to 5,200.</i>

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:18.460
<i>Why doesn't he declare his win?</i>

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:20.900
<i>It's a direct hit off the opponent!</i>

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:26.620
In the 15th turn, 
Urabe gets a tsumo win.

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.690
<i>A win, huh?</i>

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:32.290
<i>But with just pinfu, tsumo, 
iipeikou, it's only 2,700.</i>

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.370
<i>Three more turns and 
the hand ends in a draw.</i>

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:39.340
<i>Instead of taking this cheap win, 
I'll take a look at what he has instead.</i>

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:47.840
Inexplicably, both Akagi and Urabe 
ignore their winning tiles this hand.

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:49.310
It ends in a draw.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.050
I'm in tenpai.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:55.700
<i>A 5 or 8 of bamboo wait?</i>

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:59.290
<i>Urabe could have won in the 15th turn.</i>

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:03.840
<i>He ignored that on purpose just 
to take a look at our hand!</i>

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:08.160
<i>He sensed what we would want to 
avoid the most and came straight for it!</i>

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.710
All right, Akagi-han.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.670
Let's see what your riichi was made of.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.030
Show me your preemptive riichi.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.280
Sorry, I wasn't in tenpai.

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:25.970
Akagi!

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:27.780
I understand that you 
don't want to show it,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.340
but the 8,000 point penalty 
is too much right now!

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:33.400
It's cheap if you consider I'm buying 
something with these 8,000 points.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:38.290
Urabe picked up the points 
and widened the lead for now.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:42.160
I'm buying three turns in 
the future with these 8,000.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.000
Three turns in the future?

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.600
Yes, at the very end,

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:49.140
the three turns that will decide 
the outcome of the match.

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:52.430
I will advance, 
and Urabe will retreat.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.720
These 8,000 are an unavoidable expense

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:59.070
to arrange the time and place for victory.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:00.770
The foundation.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:07.120
But now the difference between 
Akagi and Urabe is 45,800.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.620
This sort of gap could 
be called hopeless.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:13.850
<i>He won't be able to turn 
the tables with tsumo wins.</i>

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.630
<i>He'll need direct hits off the opponent.</i>

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.070
<i>But if someone of Urabe's 
caliber is playing defensively,</i>

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.160
<i>you'll never be able to get a direct hit.</i>

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:24.330
<i>In a normal match,</i>

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.240
<i>Akagi wouldn't have a chance to win.</i>

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:29.520
<i>Now all he can rely on 
is a tidal wave of luck,</i>

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:33.020
<i>the kind that only comes once in 
a hundred... no, a thousand years.</i>

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:35.420
<i>A miracle surpassing all logic</i>

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:38.480
<i>that will give him baiman or yakuman hands.</i>

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:44.540
<i>What a boring starting hand.</i>

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:45.620
<i>It's lackluster.</i>

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:47.630
<i>No sign of any tidal wave.</i>

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.790
<i>That's the worst draw he could get.</i>

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:55.970
<i>Getting three foreign 
wind tiles is the worst.</i>

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:57.350
No.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:58.740
That is not the case.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.210
Actually, drawing the 
east wind was the sign.

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:06.080
A sign for the 100-year tidal wave 
that would dwarf normal waves!

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.250
<i>I don't believe it.</i>

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:14.880
<i>With just three effective draws,</i>

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.820
<i>he's already gotten so close 
to a yakuman hand.</i>

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:19.700
Eighth turn.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.560
<i>San ankou.</i>

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.350
<i>With him, I just can't 
predict what will happen.</i>

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:29.030
<i>He's far beyond the realm of ordinary men.</i>

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:32.290
<i>But now Urabe's the issue.</i>

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:33.790
<i>It looks like he's got a quick hand, too.</i>

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:34.270
Pon!

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.120
<i>He's probably in tenpai now.</i>

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.460
<i>Probably a chinitsu or honitsu of characters.</i>

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.480
<i>And probably waiting on multiple 
tiles in the lower end.</i>

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:47.850
<i>This is the moment of 
truth for Akagi's hand.</i>

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:51.390
<i>Suu ankou... Will it end as an illusion?</i>

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:54.860
<i>Or will it become reality and bear fruit?</i>

00:16:58.600 --> 00:16:59.730
<i>He got it!</i>

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:02.570
<i>Suu ankou, tenpai...</i>

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:07.670
<i>What's more, with this hand 
he can get it from a ron, too!</i>

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:10.470
<i>It's a bit risky, but he should 
discard the 4 of characters here</i>

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.110
<i>and wait on the 5 of bamboo for now.</i>

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:15.250
<i>Then he can switch to a more 
ideal single wait later.</i>

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:21.910
Riichi.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:24.810
<i>No!</i>

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.250
<i>Unthinkable!</i>

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:27.060
<i>Why a riichi?</i>

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:27.950
<i>Why?!</i>

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:29.870
<i>And why the multi-sided wait?</i>

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:32.730
<i>If anything, you'd have to go for 
the guaranteed suu ankou single wait!</i>

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:35.230
<i>Why didn't you wait 
for the 5 of bamboo?!</i>

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:37.100
Open.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:44.210
<i>I have no idea what's 
going on anymore.</i>

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:47.610
There's nothing strange about this.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.310
This is Akagi's logic.

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:50.480
Akagi's logic?

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:55.020
Akagi predicted that Urabe was waiting 
for the 1, 4, and 7 of characters.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.180
So he can't discard the 4.

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:00.450
If he can't discard it, 
he can't change his hand,

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.220
so declaring riichi isn't too strange.

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:06.650
Yes, but I don't think it 
was necessary to open it.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:10.230
Akagi figured that nobody would 
discard a 4 of characters.

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.700
Look at the discards of 
the player before him.

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:15.150
He was out of the game early on.

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:17.200
He won't discard any character tiles.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:20.240
If anybody were to do it, 
it'd be the player after him.

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.820
In the unlikely event 
that he would discard it,

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:25.230
Urabe would win because 
of the head bump rule.

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:28.300
Either way, there's no way 
that Akagi could win

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:30.190
with a ron on a 4 of characters.

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:31.940
If he can only win with a tsumo,

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:34.720
then it doesn't matter 
if his hand is open or not.

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:39.100
And that open riichi has another goal.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:40.590
Another goal?

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.060
The open suu ankou is a threat,

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.830
in order to force Urabe to compromise.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.820
Boss Kawada was half-right.

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:53.310
Urabe was indeed waiting for 
the 1, 4, and 7 of characters.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:55.070
Akagi had guessed correctly.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.350
<i>Suu ankou, open riichi?!</i>

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.820
<i>That's too crazy, you idiot!</i>

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:02.050
<i>Damn it!</i>

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:07.390
<i>If Akagi gets the suu ankou, 
he'll whip past me in one shot.</i>

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:14.120
<i>Don't tell me...</i>

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:25.100
<i>My heart can't take this.</i>

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:28.420
<i>At least he didn't get the ippatsu.</i>

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:30.490
<i>But there's no need for me 
to overextend myself here.</i>

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:31.710
Boss Kawada.

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:35.870
You don't have the silly rule here 
that dealing into an open riichi

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:38.160
will turn it into a yakuman hand, right?

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.220
No, we don't.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:41.450
<i>Then...</i>

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:42.950
<i>I'll deal into his hand!</i>

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:44.920
<i>There it is.</i>

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:46.560
<i>Is this what Akagi was aiming for?</i>

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:51.030
<i>A direct hit off his opponent 
that we thought was impossible.</i>

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:56.170
<i>So the suu ankou was a threat to 
force Urabe to discard this winning tile?</i>

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:58.620
<i>This gives him at least a mangan.</i>

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:01.120
<i>If he gets reverse dora, 
it could go up to a baiman.</i>

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:04.170
<i>While everybody is getting too 
excited in this gambling room,</i>

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:07.590
<i>he makes such a realistic 
and bold prediction.</i>

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:09.050
<i>A calculated strategy.</i>

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:14.130
<i>He's the real deal, 
without any shadow of a doubt.</i>

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:16.450
What's up?

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:18.570
Why aren't you checking 
the reverse dora?

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:19.690
Stop dreaming.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:21.710
We keep playing.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:23.530
I have no intention of 
picking up cheap points.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:28.900
A-Akagi...

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.710
Urabe took his hand 
apart to deal into mine.

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:37.890
He wrongly assumed that this deal 
would work out and defanged his hand.

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:43.620
In other words, he doesn't have 
a hand he can fight with.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:44.650
Then let's advance.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:48.380
I'll advance as much as he retreated.

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.630
I'll end him with this suu ankou.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.890
Urabe's failed plan,

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:57.490
Akagi Shigeru's uncompromising fight,

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:01.250
and the foundation for 
the next three turns...

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:05.730
Akagi closes in on Urabe, 
slowly but surely.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:19.180
Next time,  
"The Magic of Coincidence."

00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>The Magic of Coincidence</b>
